.TH BUNNYDNS 8 "2026-10-01" "bunnydns 0.1.0" "System Administration Commands"
.SH NAME
bunnydns \- inspect, edit, and reconcile Bunny DNS records
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bunnydns list
.RB [ \-H ]
.RB [ \-o
.IR field , ...]
.I zone
.br
.B bunnydns add
.I zone type name value
.RI [ property = value " ...]"
.br
.B bunnydns update
.RI "" "zone id property" = "value ..."
.br
.B bunnydns delete
.I zone id
.br
.B bunnydns apply
.RB [ \-n ]
.RB [ \-f
.IR file ]
.br
.B bunnydns verify
.RB [ \-f
.IR file ]
.br
.B bunnydns prune
.RB [ \-n ]
.RB [ \-f
.IR file ]
.br
.B bunnydns validate
.RB [ \-f
.IR file ]
.br
.B bunnydns init\-key
.br
.B bunnydns help
.br
.B bunnydns version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B bunnydns
edits records in an existing Bunny DNS zone and reconciles the records a
project declares in a records file, leaving every other record alone.
.PP
Records that a project declares carry the Bunny comment
.PP
.RS
managed-by:\fIowner\fR; key=\fIkey\fR
.RE
.PP
The owner separates projects that share a zone; the key identifies a record
through name, type, value, and TTL changes.
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
.BR list " [" \-H "] [" \-o " \fIfield\fR,...] \fIzone\fR"
List the records of
.I zone
in columns under a header.
.B \-H
omits the header and separates fields with a tab, for scripts.
.B \-o
chooses the columns from
.BR id ,
the properties below, in any order; the default is
.BR id,type,name,ttl,disabled,value .
.TP
.BI add " zone type name value " "[property=value ...]"
Add one record, read it back, and verify it.
.TP
.BI update " zone id property=value ..."
Change the given properties of one record in place.  The record keeps its
ID and its other Bunny settings, including smart routing, pull zone, script,
monitoring, and environment variables, even when its name or type changes.
.TP
.BI delete " zone id"
Delete one record, verify that it is gone, and show what it was.
.TP
.BR apply " [" \-n "] [" \-f " \fIfile\fR]"
Show the plan for the records file: one line per declared record, marked
OK, CREATE, UPDATE, or CONFLICT with a reason.  If nothing conflicts, make
the changes, read the zone again, and require every declared record to be
OK.
.B \-n
shows the plan and changes nothing.
.TP
.BR verify " [" \-f " \fIfile\fR]"
Show the plan and require every declared record to be OK.
.TP
.BR prune " [" \-n "] [" \-f " \fIfile\fR]"
After a clean verification, delete this owner's records whose keys are no
longer declared, then verify again.
.B \-n
shows what would be deleted.
.TP
.BR validate " [" \-f " \fIfile\fR]"
Check a records file without reading the key or using the network.
.TP
.B init\-key
Prompt on the terminal, with echo off, for the Bunny API key and store it
in a new mode-0600
.BR key_file .
An existing key file is never replaced.
.TP
.B help
Show the usage, properties, and settings locations.
.TP
.B version
Show the version.
.PP
Commands that take a records file read
.BI \-f " file"
or, without it, standard input.
.SH PROPERTIES
Records files and the command line use the same property names.
.TP
.B type
A, AAAA, CNAME, TXT, MX, SRV, CAA, PTR, NS, SVCB, HTTPS, or TLSA.
.TP
.B name
Zone-relative name, or
.B @
for the zone apex.  Names ignore ASCII case.
.TP
.B value
Record data.  Values are compared exactly.
.TP
.B ttl
Seconds; default 60.
.TP
.BR priority ", " weight ", " port
Integers; default 0.  Priority counts for MX, SRV, SVCB, and HTTPS, weight
for A, AAAA, and SRV, and port for SRV.
.TP
.BR flags ", " tag
CAA flags (0 to 255) and tag.
.TP
.B disabled
.B true
or
.BR false ;
default
.BR false .
.SH RECORDS FILE
A JSON object with
.B owner
(lowercase, up to 64 characters),
.B zone
(an existing Bunny DNS zone), and
.BR records ,
an array of objects.  Each record has a unique lowercase
.BR key ,
the properties above
.RB ( type ,
.BR name ,
and
.B value
are required), and optionally
.BR allow_multiple .
Unknown fields are errors.
.PP
A declared record is OK when its record matches; otherwise the record with
its ownership comment is updated, or an identical unmarked record is
adopted, or a new record is created.  A CNAME must be alone at its name,
and a new record may join an existing record set of its type only with
.BR allow_multiple .
A record marked for another owner or key, duplicate ownership comments,
ambiguous matches, and colliding declarations are conflicts, which stop
.B apply
before it writes.
.SH SETTINGS
The optional settings file holds
.IB name = value
lines and
.B #
comments:
.TP
.B key_file
The API key file.  Default: the settings directory's
.BR api\-key .
.TP
.B backup_dir
The snapshot directory.  Default:
.BR backups
in the state directory.
.PP
On SmartOS both are
.I /var/opt/scs/bunnydns
in every zone: the settings file, the API key, and the backups live together
there, apart from the installed program in
.IR /opt/scs .
Elsewhere they are
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/bunnydns
and
.IR $XDG_STATE_HOME/bunnydns ,
which default to
.I ~/.config/bunnydns
and
.IR ~/.local/state/bunnydns .
.SH SAFETY
The key file must be absolute, regular, not a symbolic link, mode 0400 or
0600, and hold one line of printable ASCII without leading or trailing
spaces.  The key never appears in a command argument or the environment; it
is sent only in the AccessKey header, only to https://api.bunny.net, and
never across a redirect.  TLS certificates are always verified.
.PP
Before any change, the zone is saved as JSON to a mode-0600 file in the
mode-0700
.BR backup_dir .
Writes are never retried and are not rolled back; keep the snapshots.  Do
not run two writers against one zone at once.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
An error, a conflict, or a failed verification.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid command-line usage.
.TP
.B 130
Interrupted by HUP, INT, or TERM.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
bunnydns init\-key
bunnydns list \-o id,type,name,priority,value example.com
bunnydns add example.com MX @ mail.example.com priority=10
bunnydns update example.com 12345 ttl=300 disabled=true
bunnydns apply \-n \-f records.json
bunnydns apply < records.json
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /var/opt/scs/bunnydns/bunnydns.conf
Settings on SmartOS.
.TP
.I /var/opt/scs/bunnydns/api\-key
Default API key file on SmartOS.
.TP
.I /var/opt/scs/bunnydns/backups
Default snapshot directory on SmartOS.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR python3 (1)
.PP
Bunny API: https://docs.bunny.net/api-reference/authentication
